      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat 3 tablespoons of olive oil in a large saucepan over medium heat. Add the onion, carrot, and celery and cook for 10 minutes until softened."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the potatoes and courgette. Stir to coat in the oil and cook for 3 minutes. Pour in the chicken stock and bring to a boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to a gentle simmer. Add the cannellini beans. Cook for 20 minutes until the potatoes are completely tender."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "While the soup simmers, make the pesto. Put the basil, pine nuts, garlic, and Parmesan in a food processor and pulse until roughly chopped. With the motor running, slowly pour in 4 tablespoons of olive oil until you have a loose paste."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the soup from the heat. Stir in about two thirds of the pesto. Taste and add more if you like. Ladle into bowls and add a final spoonful of pesto on top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The pesto turned brown",
              "cause": "The basil leaves were blitzed too long and the heat from the processor damaged them.",
              "fix": "Pulse in short bursts and keep the processor cool. Blanching the basil for 10 seconds in boiling water, then refreshing in ice water, also helps keep the colour."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The soup is too thick",
              "cause": "The potato starch has thickened the broth as it cooked.",
              "fix": "Add a splash of hot water or extra stock and stir. The soup will loosen immediately."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The pesto tastes bitter",
              "cause": "Too much garlic or the basil stalks were included.",
              "fix": "Use only the tender basil leaves and reduce the garlic to one clove. A pinch of sugar in the pesto can also balance bitterness."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This soup comes from Liguria, the thin coastal strip between the mountains and the sea. The region is famous for its basil, which grows particularly sweet in the warm coastal air. Pesto in minestrone is not optional here . it is the point of the soup. Families in Genoa make this on Sundays and eat it across the week."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
